  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In case of&nbsp;constant volume process, i.e., isochoric process,&nbsp;</p><p>From ideal gas equation,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>P</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mi>V</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo></math>constant</p><p>Or we have,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><mi>V</mi></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>273</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>If&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>273</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math></p><p>Thus, when temperature goes to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>273</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>, all constituent particles get freezed and pressure goes to zero irrespective of the density of gas.</p><p>Hence, the temperature corresponding to the point&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>273</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>.</p>"
  }
}